  "response_text": "Though it depends on the application of the OS, scheduling threads purely on priority is typically a bad idea because threads that are of low priority may never get executed if threads of higher priority that will take precedence are perpetually created by the user. These higher threads will proceed to monopolize the CPU\u2019s resources, especially because priority will be fixed. This will lead to starvation, as low priority threads won\u2019t have an opportunity to have their priority increased due to not receiving any CPU slices for an extended period of time. This it is bad since important (but not necessarily urgent) tasks will never receive any CPU time.\\n",
